course: religion-ethics
lesson: 46
title: Animals
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Animals can feel pain and fear, so most people believe we have real duties towards them, and that cruelty to animals is wrong.
explain: Christians believe God made the animals and saw that they were good, and gave people the job of caring for them, not spoiling them; St Francis of Assisi even called animals his brothers and sisters.
explain: Some questions about animals are easy: never hurt a pet for fun. Others are hard, and thoughtful people disagree: eating meat, zoos and testing medicines on animals.
explain: In a hard question the best answer gives reasons, weighs the other side and is fair to everyone, people and animals alike.
explain: For a child today this means being kind and wise: feeding your pet every day, asking an adult before you "rescue" a hurt bird, and listening to a vegetarian friend without mocking them.
fact: Animals can feel pain and fear, and cruelty to animals is wrong.
fact: Christians believe God made the animals, called them good and gave people the task of caring for them.
fact: A pet needs food, water, care, exercise and time every day: it is a responsibility, not a toy.
fact: Some people eat meat; vegetarians and vegans (who eat no animal foods at all, not even milk or eggs) do not, for reasons of animal welfare, health, the environment or religion.
fact: Whether it is right to eat meat is a disputed question; respectful people accept others' food choices.
fact: Zoos can protect endangered species and teach people, but cages limit animals' freedom; whether zoos should exist is disputed.
fact: Some medicines are tested on animals to keep people safe; whether this is right is a disputed question argued carefully on both sides; many say it should happen only when really needed, with as few animals and as little pain as possible.
fact: St Francis of Assisi called animals his brothers and sisters and, the story says, preached to the birds.
fact: If you find an injured wild bird, the wise thing is to tell an adult or call an animal rescue rather than handle it yourself.
fact: Killing insects and pests raises the question of respect for life against safety and comfort.
fact: Building on land can destroy animals' homes (habitats), so human needs and animal needs must be balanced.
exercise: Why do most people believe we have duties towards animals? | Because animals can feel pain and fear | Because animals can talk to us if we listen | Because animals are worth more than people | Because the law says so and that is all
exercise: What is the best reason for being kind to a dog? | It can feel pain and fear, and it trusts us to care for it | Because Mum said so, and that is the end of it | Because a kind dog will one day guard your house from burglars | Because people who are unkind to dogs get bitten
exercise: A family wants a puppy. What should they think about first? | Whether they have time, money and space to care for it every day | Whether the puppy will look really good in all of their holiday photos | Which colour of puppy is most popular at school | Whether the neighbours already own a bigger dog
exercise: What does "a pet is a responsibility, not a toy" mean? | A pet needs care every day, even when you are bored of it | A pet should only be played with at weekends, when there is time | A pet must be given back to the shop once it starts getting old | A pet is less important than your toys, so it can wait its turn
exercise: Your friend Ali is vegetarian. Which response to him is the most respectful? | Ask him his reasons and accept his choice, even if you eat meat | Tell him he is weird because meat is normal | Hide a bit of ham in his sandwich as a joke | Tell him he is better than everyone else who eats meat, including you
exercise: Which list gives reasons people might choose not to eat meat? | Animal welfare, health, the environment or religion | Meat is too easy to cook and much too cheap in the shops | Meat makes children grow up far too quickly and too tall | It is forbidden by the law in most countries of the world
exercise: Which is the best-reasoned view about zoos? | Good zoos protect rare animals, but cages limit freedom, so zoos must keep animals well | Zoos are bad, because I just don't like them | Zoos are fine, because people who complain about them are silly | Zoos are good, because animals have no feelings at all
exercise: Some medicines are tested on animals. Which statement describes this fairly? | It is a disputed question: human safety is weighed against animal suffering | Everybody agrees it is completely fine | Everybody agrees it is completely wrong | It has nothing to do with right and wrong
exercise: Which rule about animal testing shows the most careful thinking? | Only when really needed, with as little pain and as few animals as possible | Test anything on animals, because people matter and that is all | Never think about it, because it happens in faraway places | Let each scientist do whatever they like
exercise: Who called animals his brothers and sisters and preached to the birds? | St Francis of Assisi | St Patrick of Ireland | King David of Israel | St Paul of Tarsus
exercise: Why might Christians care about animals? | They believe God made animals good and asked people to look after them | They believe animals are gods and must be worshipped | They believe animals will stand up and judge all people at the end of time | They believe animals are only there to be used up
exercise: You find an injured bird in the garden. What is the most helpful thing to do? | Tell an adult or an animal rescue, who know how to help it | Take it to your bedroom and feed it bread | Leave it and forget about it, as wild birds are not your problem | Try to throw it into the air so it flies again
exercise: A wasp is buzzing near a baby. Which thinking is best? | Keep the baby safe first, by moving away or getting the wasp out without cruelty | Kill every wasp you ever see, because they are pests | Do nothing, because all life matters equally in every case | Tease the wasp to see what it does
exercise: Is it wrong to step on a spider just for fun? | Yes, hurting a creature for fun with no reason is cruel | No, because spiders are ugly and scary, so they don't count | No, because nobody will ever know about it, so it doesn't matter | Yes, but only if a teacher or a parent happens to see you do it
exercise: A new playground would destroy a pond full of frogs. What is the best-reasoned approach? | Look for a way to meet both needs, like building elsewhere or making a new pond | Build it at once, because children matter and frogs don't | Never build anything anywhere, because animals live everywhere | Let the children who shout loudest decide
exercise: What happens to wild animals when their habitat is built over? | They lose their homes and food, and may die out in that place | They simply move into people's houses happily and live there instead | Nothing at all, as animals do not need homes | They always grow bigger to fight the builders
test: Your little brother says: "Animals can't feel anything, so it's fine to pull the cat's tail." What should you tell him? | Animals can feel pain and fear, so pulling her tail hurts her | He is right, but only if the cat is asleep | He is right, because cats have nine lives | He is right, because the cat is ours and we can do what we like
test: Classmate: "my mum's vegan, she says ur all evil for eating burgers lol." Which reply is best? | People have good reasons on both sides; we can respect each other's choices | Your mum is mad and so are you | Vegans are always right, so I must stop today | Burgers are good, so vegans are wrong, end of
test: Sister: "Can we get a hamster? I'll look after it — well, for a week anyway." What is the most honest answer? | A hamster needs care every day for its whole life, not just a week | Fine, and after a week we can just take it back to the pet shop for a refund | Fine, hamsters can look after themselves anyway | Fine, we can keep it in a box under the bed
test: "zoos r just animal prisons, shut em all down" — what would a judge most want added? | Reasons on the other side, like saving endangered species | A louder voice and an even stronger word than "prison" for zoos | A story about a classmate who loves going to zoos far too much | Nothing, because a short answer is always the best kind of answer
test: A girl in Manchester finds a baby bird on the pavement and wants to keep it in her sock drawer. What should she do? | Ask an adult or an animal rescue what is best for the bird | Keep it in the drawer with her socks and feed it crisps every day | Put it in the freezer to keep it fresh | Take it to school to show her class
test: Your friend says, "St Francis said animals don't matter, only people." How would you put it right? | St Francis called animals his brothers and sisters | St Francis said only birds matter, not other animals | St Francis said people should not talk about animals | St Francis said animals must obey people in everything
test: "Testing medicine on animals is wrong cos the scientists are horrible people." What is weak about this argument? | It attacks people instead of giving reasons about the question | It is too long and has too many reasons | It mentions medicine, which is completely off the topic of animals | It is weak because it agrees with the teacher
test: A judge asks: "Is it ever right to kill an insect?" Which answer is best-reasoned? | Sometimes, for safety or health, but killing just for fun is wrong | Never, because every single insect matters exactly as much as a person | Always, because insects are tiny and don't count | I don't know, because it's an insect question
test: "shut up about the frogs, we want the bloody skate park" — what is this speaker ignoring? | The animals' needs and the chance of a fair solution for both | The cost of the skate ramps, the fences and the paint for the new park | The rules of skateboarding at the park | Nothing, because they are just in a bad mood
test: Which statement about eating meat is true? | It is a disputed question that thoughtful people answer differently | Every religion in the world forbids eating any kind of meat completely | Every scientist agrees meat is wrong to eat | No one has ever chosen not to eat meat
test: A Christian girl and a Hindu boy both care for animals. What might they share? | A belief that animals deserve kindness and respect | A belief that pets should always be kept locked in cages | A belief that only pets have feelings, not farm animals | A belief that wild animals don't matter at all to anyone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the grounds upon which duties towards animals are commonly held to rest? | Animals are capable of experiencing pain and fear, and are therefore owed humane treatment | Animals are possessed of the faculty of speech and may assert their own entitlements | Animals are of greater worth than human beings in every respect | Animals are owed nothing whatsoever save that which statute expressly requires
exam: It being given that the keeping of animals in zoological gardens is a disputed matter, select the response that is most consistent with a well-reasoned position. | The conservation of endangered species is to be weighed against the restriction of the animals' liberty | Zoological gardens are to be abolished forthwith, the matter admitting of no other view | Zoological gardens are beyond criticism, the animals being incapable of suffering | The question is to be determined by the personal preference of the most vocal party
exam: Identify the course of action most appropriate where a child discovers an injured wild bird upon the premises. | The matter is to be referred to a responsible adult or to an animal rescue service | The bird is to be retained indoors and nourished upon bread by the child who found it | The bird is to be disregarded, wild creatures being no concern of the finder | The bird is to be launched into the air in order that it may resume flight
exam: Whereas a proposed recreational facility would occasion the destruction of a pond inhabited by frogs, determine the response that best exemplifies sound ethical reasoning. | The needs of both children and animals are to be considered and a solution serving both sought | The facility is to be constructed without delay, animals being of no account whatsoever in the matter | All construction whatsoever is to be prohibited in perpetuity | The decision is to be made by whichever party raises its voice the loudest
